# Code owners for Blockcast/paperclip.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT THIS FILE DOES, AND WHAT IT DOES NOT DO. Read this before editing.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# GitHub does NOT enforce CODEOWNERS on this repository. `master` requires zero
# approving reviews, so `require_code_owner_reviews` has nothing to attach to
# and no entry below is a merge gate. What an entry buys is an auto-requested
# review from the named owner, which the landing routine then holds the PR on
# rather than enqueuing it. That is a hold plus a named reviewer — not
# guaranteed scrutiny, and not a blocking check. Tracking: PEN-5119.
#
# Measure that claim on a PULL REQUEST, not on the branch-protection routes.
# The discriminating token is the PRESENCE of REVIEW_REQUIRED over a census of
# open PRs BASED ON the protected branch — never the ABSENCE of a value over a
# recent-activity sample. On a repository with no requirement `reviewDecision`
# still reads APPROVED as soon as any write-access approval lands, so a sample
# that happens to catch approved PRs comes back populated, confident and
# uninformative; and a PR based on a feature branch reports on THAT base, not
# on the protected one. Measured 2026-11-12, full censuses with completeness
# asserted: of 70 open PRs based on `master` here, REVIEW_REQUIRED 0 (APPROVED
# 8, empty 62), against 145 of 146 based on `main` in `penstock-llm-proxy-core`,
# where the requirement IS live. The two protection routes readable without
# `administration` — `repos/:o/:r/branches/:b` and
# `repos/:o/:r/rules/branches/:b` — report NO review requirement for BOTH of
# those repositories, so they cannot tell the two states apart.
#
# Do not carry a `reviewDecision` reading across repositories: its mapping to
# configuration is repo-dependent. On `Blockcast/onprem-k8s`, whose ruleset sets
# `required_approving_review_count: 0`, the field reads empty whether or not an
# approval exists at the head (measured 2026-11-12). Run the census on the
# repository you are asking about, with a control repository in the same window.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# VERIFYING AN EDIT, AND THE ONE THING THE CHECK CANNOT SEE.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# GitHub silently ignores any entry naming an account without write access to
# this repository. Such a rule parses fine and enforces nothing — no
# auto-requested review, no hold. Catch that with:
#
#   gh api "repos/Blockcast/paperclip/codeowners/errors?ref=master"   # expect {"errors":[]}
#
# That is ALL this check answers: every owner RESOLVES and has write access. It
# says nothing about whether that owner ever reviews, so `{"errors":[]}` cannot
# tell a working owner from an inert one and is not evidence a rule does
# anything. Measured 2026-11-12: `penstock-llm-proxy-core` owns `*` with
# `@Blockcast/onprem-k8s-ally-reviewer @kkroo` and returns `{"errors":[]}`,
# while the team arm satisfies nothing there: measured 2026-11-07, 12 of 12
# approvals by `allyblockcast` — a member of that owning team — left
# `kkroo`-authored PRs at REVIEW_REQUIRED, and @kkroo carried 100% of the
# satisfactions (PEN-5119 rev 15).
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CHOOSING AN OWNER.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Replace @kkroo if a different maintainer should own a surface. Do NOT
# substitute an org team for a human on the assumption that a team is
# equivalent — that is the proxy-core configuration above, and a team can
# satisfy the `errors` check while contributing zero reviews.
#
# There is NO single-query test here of whether a candidate owner reviews, and
# this file is the wrong place to invent one. `reviewDecision` cannot do it:
# with zero required reviews ANY write-access approval flips it to APPROVED, so
# an inert owner passes — the same shape as the `errors` check two sections up.
# A `reviewed-by:` search fails the other way round: it counts GitHub review
# OBJECTS, and this repository's working owner stopped producing those on
# ~2026-10-29 and signs off as a PR COMMENT instead (`code-owner sign-off:
# <40-hex>`; 47 PRs carried one between 2026-10-23 and 2026-11-11, 43 of them
# merged — PEN-5119 rev 19). A query that counts only review objects returns
# zero for the one owner we have measured to work.
#
# So judge a candidate on EVIDENCE, not on a check: a record of code-owner acts
# on THIS repository, in EITHER channel — submitted reviews or sign-off
# comments at the head — and the penstock measurement above as the thing to
# reason from. Say in the PR which evidence you looked at.
#
# Keep co-owners on ONE line per rule: CODEOWNERS is last-match-wins, so a
# second line for the same pattern REPLACES the first instead of adding to it.

.github/** @kkroo
scripts/release*.sh @kkroo
scripts/release-*.mjs @kkroo
scripts/create-github-release.sh @kkroo
scripts/rollback-latest.sh @kkroo
doc/RELEASING.md @kkroo
doc/PUBLISHING.md @kkroo
doc/RELEASE-AUTOMATION-SETUP.md @kkroo
skills/** @kkroo

# Package files — dependency changes require review
# package.json matches recursively at all depths (covers root + all workspaces)
package.json @kkroo
pnpm-lock.yaml @kkroo
pnpm-workspace.yaml @kkroo
.npmrc @kkroo

# Secret-scan configuration — the files that can SILENCE the scanner (PEN-6393).
#
# `.github/**` above owns the `secret_scan` lane that RUNS gitleaks. These entries
# own the files that can switch it off, which that rule does not reach: gitleaks
# resolves its config from the working tree, so an allowlist entry added by a PR
# takes effect on that same PR, and the lane's own failure comment tells the
# author to add one. Review of that diff is the only thing enforcing the policy
# `.gitleaks.toml`'s header states.
#
# `.gitleaksignore` is a second silencing channel that needs no config edit:
# gitleaks v8.28.0 — the version the lane pins — auto-loads it from `--source`
# and drops findings by fingerprint. No such file exists yet; the rule is here so
# the PR that first adds one draws a reviewer. `.gitleaks-staging.toml` is the
# warn-only staging config (PEN-6280) and is likewise not on master yet.
#
# Listed by exact name rather than a glob: a config under a NEW name is not
# covered — add a line here when you add one.
#
# What these entries buy is what the header at the top of this file describes:
# an auto-requested review plus a landing hold, not a merge gate.
.gitleaks.toml @kkroo
.gitleaks-staging.toml @kkroo
.gitleaksignore @kkroo
